.TH FSCK.FAT 8
.SH NAME
fsck.fat \- check and repair FAT16 and FAT32 filesystems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fsck.fat
[
.B \-fnpvy
]
.I filesystem
[
.I filesystem ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fsck.fat
checks FAT copies, cluster chains, directories, lost clusters, and FAT32
FSInfo metadata.
The filesystem must not be mounted read-write while it is checked.
.PP
The FAT32 FSInfo next-free field is a search hint.  An unknown value or any
cluster number inside the volume is valid even when that cluster is currently
allocated; only an out-of-range value is repaired.
.PP
For a FAT32 filesystem whose boot sector declares more sectors than the
backing block device or image contains,
.B fsck.fat
uses the device boundary for all reads and checks the excluded FAT entries and
all links crossing that boundary.  It offers to reduce the primary and backup
boot-sector geometry only when every excluded cluster represented by the FAT
is free, every remaining excluded cluster is outside the FAT itself, and no
FAT chain crosses the boundary.  It never relocates allocated clusters.
Geometry repair requires an interactive confirmation or
.BR \-y ;
preen mode leaves it unresolved.
.PP
The checker uses a compact allocation bitmap and reads the FAT a sector at a
time, so its memory use is suitable for small ReBSD systems.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Open the filesystem read-only and answer no to all repair questions.
.TP
.B \-y
Answer yes to all repair questions.
.TP
.B \-p
Preen mode.  Apply repairs marked safe and leave destructive choices
unresolved.
.TP
.B \-f
Force a full check.  A full check is currently always performed.
.TP
.B \-v
Print additional volume information.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero means the filesystem was clean.
One means errors were corrected.
Four means errors remain uncorrected.
Eight means an operational error prevented the check.
.SH SEE ALSO
fsck(8), fsck.ufs(8), mount(8)
